question.:question/worked-solution:
The vectors are coplanar if and only if their scalar triple product is zero. Expanding along the first row gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\begin{vmatrix}0&3&4\\2&-2&-4\\-3&0&k^{2}\end{vmatrix}\\&=(0)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-2&-4\\0&k^{2}\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad -(3)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}2&-4\\-3&k^{2}\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad +(4)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}2&-2\\-3&0\end{vmatrix}\\&=(0)\cdot ((-2)\cdot (k^{2})-(-4)\cdot (0))\\&\quad -(3)\cdot ((2)\cdot (k^{2})-(-4)\cdot (-3))\\&\quad +(4)\cdot ((2)\cdot (0)-(-2)\cdot (-3))\\&=(0)\cdot (- 2 k^{2})\\&\quad -(3)\cdot (2 k^{2} - 12)\\&\quad +(4)\cdot (-6)\\&=12 - 6 k^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Set the triple product equal to zero and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
12 - 6 k^{2}&=0\\- 6 k^{2}&=-12\\k^{2}&=2\\k&=\pm \sqrt{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Both signs are necessary because both have square $2$. Thus the vectors are coplanar exactly when $k=-\sqrt{2}$ or $k=\sqrt{2}$.

