question.:question/worked-solution:
Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. The outer product and the exponential chain rule both matter. First differentiate the exponential input:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dx}\left(x y\right)&=\frac{dy}{dx} x + y\\
\frac{d}{dx}\left(e^{x y}\right)&=\left(\frac{dy}{dx} x + y\right) e^{x y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
&\frac{d}{dx}\left(- 2 x e^{x y} + y^{2}\right)=\frac{d}{dx}\left(1 - 2 e\right)\\
&2 \frac{dy}{dx} y - 2 x \left(\frac{dy}{dx} x + y\right) e^{x y} - 2 e^{x y}=0\\
&\left(- 2 x^{2} e^{x y} + 2 y\right)\frac{dy}{dx}=-\left(- 2 x y e^{x y} - 2 e^{x y}\right)\\
&\frac{dy}{dx}=-\frac{- 2 x y e^{x y} - 2 e^{x y}}{- 2 x^{2} e^{x y} + 2 y}
\end{aligned}
$$

At $(1,1)$, the coefficient of $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $2 - 2 e\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}
m&=\left.\frac{dy}{dx}\right|_{(1,1)}\\
&=-\frac{- 4 e}{2 - 2 e}\\
&=\frac{2 e}{1 - e}.
\end{aligned}
$$

