The plane fixes $x$, so the section slope is $f_y(1,2)$. Hold $x$ constant. The logarithm contributes $2 x/y$. For the quotient, differentiate its exponential using the chain factor $x$, then subtract the numerator times the derivative of the denominator. Simplify the rational coefficients and keep the evaluated exponential input:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_y(x,y)&=\frac{2 x}{y}+-2\frac{y(xe^{x y})-e^{x y}}{y^2} \\
&=\frac{2 x}{y}+\frac{-2e^{x y}(x y-1)}{y^2} \\
m=f_y(1,2)&=\frac{2}{2}+\frac{-2e^{2}(2-1)}{(2)^2} \\
&=1-\frac{1}{2}e^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

The plane fixes $y$, so the section slope is $f_x(2,1)$. Hold $y$ constant. The logarithm contributes $2 y/x$. For the quotient, differentiate its exponential using the chain factor $2 y$, then subtract the numerator times the derivative of the denominator. Simplify the rational coefficients and keep the evaluated exponential input:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x(x,y)&=\frac{2 y}{x}+2\frac{x(2 ye^{2 x y})-e^{2 x y}}{x^2} \\
&=\frac{2 y}{x}+\frac{2e^{2 x y}(2 x y-1)}{x^2} \\
m=f_x(2,1)&=\frac{2}{2}+\frac{2e^{4}(4-1)}{(2)^2} \\
&=1+\frac{3}{2}e^{4}
\end{aligned}
$$

Use the scalar line-integral formula

$$
\int_C f\,\mathrm{d}s=\int_a^b f(\mathbf r(t))\,\|\mathbf r'(t)\|\,\mathrm{d}t.
$$

Differentiate both coordinates and compute the speed:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf r'(t)&=\langle -2, -2\rangle \\
\|\mathbf r'(t)\|&=\sqrt{(-2)^2+(-2)^2} \\
&=2 \sqrt{2}
\end{aligned}
$$



Substitute the coordinates into the density:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(\mathbf r(t))&=\frac{2}{(- 2 t - 3)-(- 2 t)} \\
&=\frac{2}{-3}=- \frac{2}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Multiply by the speed and integrate, subtracting the lower endpoint:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_C f\,\mathrm{d}s&=\int_{1}^{4} \left(- \frac{2}{3}\right)\cdot (2 \sqrt{2})\,\mathrm{d}t \\
&=\int_{1}^{4} - \frac{4 \sqrt{2}}{3}\,\mathrm{d}t \\
&=\left[- \frac{4 \sqrt{2} t}{3}\right]_{1}^{4} \\
&=\left(- \frac{16 \sqrt{2}}{3}\right)-\left(- \frac{4 \sqrt{2}}{3}\right) \\
&=- 4 \sqrt{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

